(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-general-dermatology-i-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"General Dermatology I Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 4-month-old infant has weeping inflammatory patches and crusted plaques on the cheeks, scalp and extensor surfaces of the arms. The mother reports the baby rubs against bedding rather than scratching. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Irritant contact dermatitis","Atopic dermatitis","Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Nummular eczema"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The infantile pattern favours cheeks, scalp and extensor surfaces, and rubbing replaces scratching at this age. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman noticed a single 3 cm oval pink patch on her back ten days ago; the centre has since cleared leaving a fine rim of scale. Now she has many smaller similar oval lesions across her trunk, their long axes running along the skin cleavage lines. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tinea corporis","Nummular eczema","Pityriasis rosea","Guttate psoriasis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The herald patch with a collarette of scale, followed 1 to 2 weeks later by lesions in a Christmas tree pattern, is the picture. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 169"},{"q":"A 6-year-old with known atopic dermatitis develops painful, uniform punched-out erosions over a flaring area, with fever and malaise. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Increase the potency of the topical corticosteroid","Begin a topical calcineurin inhibitor","Order patch testing","Order herpes simplex virus polymerase chain reaction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Painful monomorphic erosions with systemic illness is the stated trigger for this test, since eczema herpeticum needs excluding before treatment escalates. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has widespread tense bullae and a clinical suspicion of bullous pemphigoid. Which test confirms the diagnosis?","choices":["Potassium hydroxide preparation of the blister roof","Tzanck smear of a fresh blister","Bacterial culture of blister fluid","Biopsy of perilesional tissue for direct immunofluorescence"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — lesional tissue goes for histopathology and perilesional tissue for immunofluorescence, with serum studies or ELISA identifying anti-basement membrane zone antibodies. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with perioral dermatitis has stopped her topical corticosteroid. Which is the most appropriate counselling point about what happens next?","choices":["The eruption may temporarily worsen after corticosteroid withdrawal before it improves","The eruption should clear completely within 48 hours of stopping","She should resume the steroid if the rash worsens at all","The condition will not recur once the corticosteroid has been stopped once"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — telling her in advance is what stops her restarting the steroid. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman has intensely itchy, flat-topped violaceous papules on both wrists and ankles, some coalescing into plaques with fine white lines across the surface. She also has lacey white patches on her inner cheeks. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Psoriasis","Pemphigus vulgaris","Lichen planus","Lichen simplex chronicus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the four Ps (pruritic, purple, polygonal papules or plaques) with Wickham's striae (fine white surface lines) and buccal involvement. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 176"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with recurrent palmar vesicles asks how to reduce flares. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Wash the hands frequently with an antibacterial soap through the course of the day, in order to prevent secondary bacterial infection of the ruptured vesicles","Use hot water to relieve the itching","Apply emollients only when the skin feels dry","Avoid irritants such as detergents, solvents and hair dyes, wash with lukewarm water and soap-free cleanser, dry hands thoroughly, and apply emollients immediately afterwards"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — trigger avoidance and barrier care are the named education points. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 9-year-old presents with a sudden shower of small raindrop-shaped scaly pink papules over the trunk and proximal limbs. He had a sore throat about two weeks ago. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Nummular eczema","Guttate psoriasis","Pityriasis rosea","Plaque psoriasis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — raindrop-shaped papules in a child 2 to 3 weeks after streptococcal infection or an upper respiratory infection. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 160"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy is brought in with a recurrent, intensely pruritic rash in the antecubital and popliteal fossae. Examination shows poorly demarcated erythematous plaques with excoriations and lichenification (thickening with exaggerated skin lines). His mother has asthma. There is no fever, drainage, crusting or tenderness. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Oral cephalexin","Oral terbinafine","Systemic prednisone for four weeks with a slow taper","Topical hydrocortisone with regular emollient use"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Low-potency steroid plus barrier repair is first line, and the flexural distribution with a family atopy history is classic childhood atopic dermatitis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"An 82-year-old woman has had several weeks of intense itching with urticarial plaques but no blisters. She is otherwise well. Which is the most likely explanation?","choices":["The prodromal phase of bullous pemphigoid, which may precede bullae by weeks to months","Chronic idiopathic urticaria entirely unrelated to any blistering disease","Early pemphigus vulgaris","Lichen simplex chronicus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the prodrome presents as pruritic urticarial or oedematous lesions before the blistering phase. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has coin-shaped scaly plaques on both shins. A potassium hydroxide preparation is negative and there is no purulence. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Oral terbinafine","Compression therapy","Ketoconazole shampoo applied to all the affected areas","Medium to high potency topical corticosteroids with emollients"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — steroids for the active lesions and emollients to restore the barrier and prevent recurrence. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man with recurrent greasy scaling of the scalp and nasolabial folds asks how long treatment will be needed. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The condition is chronic and relapsing, so repeated and long-term use of medication is often required","A single two-week course of antifungal shampoo is usually curative and needs no repeating","It will resolve permanently once stress is controlled","It is contagious and household members should be treated as well"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — that expectation is stated explicitly for this condition. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has recurrent scaling of the scalp, eyebrows, nasolabial folds and external ears. Examination shows greasy, yellow-white scale over mildly erythematous poorly demarcated patches. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Oral terbinafine","High-potency topical corticosteroids as monotherapy","Topical mupirocin","Ketoconazole shampoo and topical ketoconazole cream"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Topical antifungals are the mainstay because Malassezia overgrowth drives the condition; steroids may be added early to reduce inflammation. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"An 80-year-old woman has bullous pemphigoid limited to a few areas of the trunk. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Low-dose methotrexate with folic acid","Ultrapotent topical steroids","Oral prednisone with azathioprine","Rituximab"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — mild disease is managed topically, with oral prednisone or doxycycline reserved for moderate to severe disease. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"A 22-year-old presents two days after clearing brush in the garden with intensely itchy blisters in streaks across both forearms. Examination shows linear vesicles and papules in several stages, with excoriations. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Allergic contact dermatitis from urushiol","Irritant contact dermatitis of the hands","Herpes zoster","Bullous pemphigoid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The linear distribution comes from grazing the plant in passing, and lesions erupt 4 to 96 hours after exposure. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 9-month-old has a bright red rash in the diaper area. Examination shows beefy erythema involving the inguinal folds with peripheral scale and scattered satellite papules and pustules beyond the main area. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Bacterial diaper dermatitis","Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Candidal diaper dermatitis","Irritant diaper dermatitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fold involvement with satellite lesions is the candidal pattern; irritant disease spares the folds. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A mother of a child newly diagnosed with atopic dermatitis is anxious about using topical steroids at all. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Advise applying the steroid generously over the whole body twice daily","Advise that the condition resolves permanently by school age, so consistent daily treatment is optional and can be stopped whenever the skin looks clear","Address the concern directly, review the side effects, demonstrate how much to apply, and provide a written flare and infection action plan","Advise avoiding all topical steroids and relying on emollients alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Addressing steroid concerns directly and demonstrating quantity and application are named education points. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman with rapidly progressive alopecia areata is distressed and withdrawing socially. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The hair loss is permanent, because the follicle and its stem cells have been destroyed","The condition is contagious to household members","Nail changes exclude the diagnosis","Psychological support is one of the most important considerations, and support groups may be offered"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is named among the most important factors in managing this condition. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 140"},{"q":"A 46-year-old with known plaque psoriasis stops a course of oral prednisone prescribed elsewhere. Days later she develops fever for several days, then an abrupt generalised eruption of 2 to 3 mm pustules over the trunk and limbs including the palms and nail beds, some coalescing into lakes of pus. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Recognise this as pustular psoriasis of the Von Zumbusch type and treat it as a life-threatening emergency","Begin oral antibiotics for a presumed bacterial pustular infection","Restart the oral prednisone at the previous dose and then continue it indefinitely thereafter","Reassure and review in two weeks, since psoriasis flares are expected"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is abrupt and life-threatening, may follow systemic steroid withdrawal, and is commonly mistaken for bacterial or viral infection. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 160"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with long-standing venous insufficiency has itchy, hyperpigmented plaques over both gaiter regions with induration. His ankle-brachial index is normal. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Compression therapy, with leg elevation, walking and calf-muscle exercises","Long-term systemic antibiotics","High-potency topical corticosteroids as the sole long-term therapy","Strict bed rest with the legs dependent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — compression is the cornerstone once arterial circulation is adequate. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 117"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man with plaque psoriasis develops new plaques along a surgical scar several weeks after an operation. Which phenomenon does this represent?","choices":["Wickham's striae","Koebner's phenomenon","The Auspitz sign","A positive Nikolsky sign"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the development of lesions at sites of trauma, seen in psoriasis and also in lichen planus. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 159"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man has patchy hair loss with an unusual scarred appearance in one area and diffuse atypical loss elsewhere, and the picture remains unclear after dermoscopy. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Repeat dermoscopy in six months","Scalp biopsy","Begin intralesional corticosteroids empirically","Begin topical minoxidil"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — biopsy is performed when scarring alopecia, diffuse atypical loss or persistent diagnostic uncertainty remains after clinical and dermoscopic assessment. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 139"},{"q":"A 9-year-old with guttate psoriasis following a streptococcal throat infection is otherwise well. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Begin a biologic agent such as etanercept, adalimumab, infliximab or ixekizumab","No specific treatment is required, though phototherapy or topical steroids may be used","Begin methotrexate","Begin acitretin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — guttate psoriasis often needs no treatment. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 164"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has a single intensely itchy, well-demarcated thickened plaque on her lateral ankle with exaggerated skin markings and pigment change. She admits to scratching it most evenings while watching television. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Break the itch-scratch cycle through education, emollients, behavioural substitution and nail care, with a limited course of a potent topical steroid","Excise the plaque","Begin oral antibiotics","Begin an oral antifungal such as terbinafine, on the basis that the thickened scaly plaque is most likely a fungal infection of long standing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — recurrence is common unless the initiating itch is controlled. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 183"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has burning and small red papules around her mouth. Examination shows grouped monomorphic papules and pustules over the nasolabial folds and chin, sparing a narrow rim next to the vermilion border. She has used hydrocortisone cream daily for several months. Which is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["Apply an occlusive moisturiser several times each day instead","Discontinue the corticosteroid and simplify her skin-care routine","Increase the potency of the topical corticosteroid","Begin topical adapalene"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Topical corticosteroid exposure is the most important modifiable association, and continued use perpetuates the condition. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man wishes to treat male-pattern hair loss and asks about finasteride. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["It is most effective at the frontal hairline","It can safely be shared with a female partner who is also experiencing hair thinning at the crown","About 2% of men report reduced libido and erectile function, and these effects reverse when the drug is stopped","The sexual side effects are permanent once they occur"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It works best combined with minoxidil, and it is not indicated in women and contraindicated in pregnancy. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 148"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has a glazed, well-demarcated dermatitis over both eyelids that began after she started using a new cosmetic. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Perioral dermatitis","Irritant contact dermatitis","Atopic dermatitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the eyelids and face are among the commonest sites, from makeup and masks, and the glazed well-demarcated appearance fits. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has a six-week history of painful mouth erosions and now has flaccid blisters on the scalp and chest that rupture easily and bleed. Lateral pressure on adjacent normal skin causes the top layer to slough. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Lichen planus","Pemphigus vulgaris","Bullous pemphigoid","Erythema multiforme"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Oral onset, flaccid easily ruptured bullae and a positive Nikolsky sign are the triad, reflecting intraepithelial acantholysis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 130"},{"q":"A 26-year-old developed an itchy rash confined to a rectangle on her forearm, sparing a small central area, three days after a minor injury. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The reaction indicates the wound is infected and needs antibiotics","Future exposure to the same adhesive will not provoke a reaction once this episode has settled","The reaction is to the adhesive in the dressing, and the spared centre corresponds to the cotton pad","The rash is contagious and she should avoid close contact until it clears"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the shape reproducing the object is the hallmark of allergic contact dermatitis to adhesives. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has several round scaly plaques on his forearms. The lesions are uniform throughout without central clearing, and he has been applying an over-the-counter antifungal for three weeks without improvement. Which is the best initial diagnostic test?","choices":["Potassium hydroxide preparation","Punch biopsy for direct immunofluorescence","Serum immunoglobulin E level","Bacterial culture and sensitivity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tinea corporis is the main differential, and it must be excluded before committing to a diagnosis of nummular eczema. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 74"}]};
